      </p>

      <div class="ed-body prose prose-lg">
        <p class="ed-lead">
          Most people asking this question want a number, so here it is: about 30 minutes of
          brisk walking on five days a week meets the core US activity guideline for adults. That
          is the floor, not the ceiling. How far above it you should go depends on what you want
          from the treadmill, how fit you are now, and how hard those minutes actually are.
        </p>

        <aside class="ed-note not-prose">
          <span class="ed-note-k">What we checked</span>
          <strong class="text-gray-900">What this page is, and is not.</strong> We review
          treadmills. This page summarises mainstream public-health guidance so you can plan
          treadmill sessions sensibly — it is not medical advice, coaching or a weight-loss
          programme. If you have a heart or lung condition, diabetes, joint problems, are
          pregnant, take medication that affects heart rate or balance, or have been inactive for a
          long time, check with a doctor before increasing your activity. <strong class="text-gray-900">On the figures below:</strong> session lengths, calorie
          costs and step counts are typical ranges from public guidance and standard estimation
          equations, not measurements of any individual.
        </aside>

        <nav class="ed-toc not-prose" aria-label="In this guide">
          <h2>In this guide</h2>
          <ol>
            <li><a href="#short-answer">The Short Answer</a></li>
            <li><a href="#guidelines">What the Guidelines Actually Say</a></li>
            <li><a href="#by-goal">How Long to Walk, by Goal</a></li>
            <li><a href="#weight-loss">Can You Lose Weight Walking on a Treadmill?</a></li>
            <li><a href="#make-it-moderate">How Fast and Steep to Make the Minutes Count</a></li>
            <li><a href="#beginners">A Beginner Progression</a></li>
            <li><a href="#splitting">Does It Have to Be All at Once?</a></li>
            <li><a href="#steps">Steps Versus Minutes</a></li>
            <li><a href="#too-long">How Long Is Too Long?</a></li>
            <li><a href="#desk-walking">What About Under-Desk Walking?</a></li>
            <li><a href="#verdict">The Verdict</a></li>
            <li><a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
          </ol>
